  "name": "Question 15",
  "problem": "Given that $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{x}{2})$ is defined for $x\\in (-2\\pi ,0),$ find the values of $x$ for which the function is strictly increasing.",
  "worked_solution": "The function is strictly increasing where ${f}^{\u2032}(x)>0.$ Differentiating $f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\frac{x}{2}),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\frac{x}{2})>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the domain is $x\\in (-2\\pi ,0),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2\\pi  & <x<0 \\\\ -\\pi  & <\\frac{x}{2}<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that cosine is positive in the first and the fourth quadrant. So, this reduces to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\pi }{2} & <\\frac{x}{2}<0 \\\\ -\\pi  & <x<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x)$ is strictly increasing on the interval $-\\pi <x<0.$",
